1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,580
In October 2017, a steel Rolex sells for

2
00:00:03,580 --> 00:00:06,040
seventeen point eight million dollars.

3
00:00:06,040 --> 00:00:09,320
It belongs to the story of a movie star, a racing driver,

4
00:00:10,020 --> 00:00:11,760
and a young man working on a treehouse.

5
00:00:12,760 --> 00:00:15,360
Why did Paul Newman's Rolex sell for so much?

6
00:00:16,120 --> 00:00:17,900
The answer begins with a gift.

7
00:00:18,820 --> 00:00:19,740
The watch has no diamonds.

8
00:00:20,620 --> 00:00:22,180
Its case is stainless steel.

9
00:00:23,040 --> 00:00:26,080
It is a chronograph: a watch with a stopwatch built in.

10
00:00:26,720 --> 00:00:29,440
You can start it, stop it, and measure an interval.

11
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:31,660
There is useful machinery here.

12
00:00:32,320 --> 00:00:35,060
But useful machinery alone doesn't explain that number.

13
00:00:35,740 --> 00:00:38,420
To understand it, follow the watch through the hands of

14
00:00:38,420 --> 00:00:39,500
the people who wore it.

15
00:00:40,240 --> 00:00:43,220
Before the bidding, before the collectors, there was a

16
00:00:43,220 --> 00:00:45,800
husband whose wife wanted him to drive carefully.

17
00:00:47,680 --> 00:00:50,440
Rolex introduced the Cosmograph in 1963.

18
00:00:51,640 --> 00:00:54,020
The Daytona name tied the watch to motor racing.

19
00:00:54,760 --> 00:00:56,320
A driver might want to measure a lap.

20
00:00:56,920 --> 00:00:59,620
A crew member might want to compare one run with another.

21
00:01:00,400 --> 00:01:03,120
The chronograph gives them an interval; the scale around

22
00:01:03,120 --> 00:01:06,340
the bezel can turn a measured interval into an average

23
00:01:06,340 --> 00:01:07,940
speed over a known distance.

24
00:01:08,760 --> 00:01:12,316
For example, one mile in thirty seconds works out to one

25
00:01:12,316 --> 00:01:13,440
hundred and twenty miles per hour.

26
00:01:14,620 --> 00:01:17,700
That's a calculation, not a story about Newman setting a

27
00:01:17,700 --> 00:01:21,120
lap record. The idea is easy to grasp: make

28
00:01:21,120 --> 00:01:22,540
time something you can use.

29
00:01:23,380 --> 00:01:25,760
Newman's watch was a reference six two three nine.

30
00:01:26,980 --> 00:01:27,940
You wound it by hand.

31
00:01:28,580 --> 00:01:31,660
A small mechanical routine, repeated day after day.

32
00:01:32,500 --> 00:01:35,940
Then a very personal message changed the meaning of that

33
00:01:35,940 --> 00:01:36,740
racing instrument.

34
00:01:38,640 --> 00:01:42,080
Joanne Woodward, Newman's wife and fellow actor, gave him

35
00:01:42,080 --> 00:01:45,560
the Daytona. The back carried four words:

36
00:01:45,760 --> 00:01:49,080
“Drive Carefully Me.” The wording is

37
00:01:49,080 --> 00:01:52,200
wonderfully compact. A driving instruction,

38
00:01:52,200 --> 00:01:54,160
followed by a personal sign-off.

39
00:01:55,120 --> 00:01:58,020
Their film Winning connected Newman with the world of

40
00:01:58,020 --> 00:02:01,460
racing, and his interest continued beyond the production.

41
00:02:01,880 --> 00:02:04,680
The watch fit that new part of his life.

42
00:02:06,020 --> 00:02:08,780
Think of the difference between a gift in a shop window

43
00:02:08,780 --> 00:02:11,160
and a gift after years of use.

44
00:02:12,220 --> 00:02:13,500
In the window, you see a model.

45
00:02:14,460 --> 00:02:16,420
Once someone has worn it, you begin to see a

46
00:02:16,420 --> 00:02:19,880
relationship. The inscription gives that relationship a

47
00:02:19,880 --> 00:02:21,380
physical place to live.

48
00:02:21,380 --> 00:02:24,760
And racing was about to supply much more than a glamorous

49
00:02:24,760 --> 00:02:28,340
backdrop. Newman went from playing a driver to

50
00:02:28,340 --> 00:02:29,540
competing against them.

51
00:02:31,640 --> 00:02:35,160
In 1979, Newman raced at the twenty-four hours of

52
00:02:35,160 --> 00:02:38,700
Le Mans. He shared a Porsche nine thirty-five with

53
00:02:38,700 --> 00:02:40,840
Dick Barbour and Rolf Stommelen.

54
00:02:41,360 --> 00:02:44,460
They finished second overall and won their class.

55
00:02:45,520 --> 00:02:49,020
Second overall. In a race that lasted a full day.

56
00:02:49,840 --> 00:02:52,600
That is a useful answer to anyone tempted to treat the

57
00:02:52,600 --> 00:02:56,000
racing as a movie-star hobby with a camera

58
00:02:56,000 --> 00:02:59,440
nearby. The achievement belongs to a team, and

59
00:02:59,440 --> 00:03:00,960
the watch wasn't driving the car.

60
00:03:01,600 --> 00:03:03,660
But it gives the wider story substance.

61
00:03:04,760 --> 00:03:07,240
Newman had entered the world that the chronograph was

62
00:03:07,240 --> 00:03:10,540
designed to serve. Now imagine seeing photographs of an

63
00:03:10,540 --> 00:03:13,680
actor you recognize, in a setting where timing matters,

64
00:03:13,680 --> 00:03:16,020
with a distinctive watch on his wrist.

65
00:03:16,900 --> 00:03:18,860
The association becomes easy to remember.

66
00:03:19,740 --> 00:03:23,020
Soon his name would help people identify an entire kind

67
00:03:23,020 --> 00:03:25,900
of Daytona. Which creates a surprisingly important

68
00:03:25,900 --> 00:03:27,020
problem for our story.

69
00:03:28,920 --> 00:03:32,460
A Paul Newman Daytona does not necessarily mean a Daytona

70
00:03:32,460 --> 00:03:33,900
owned by Paul Newman.

71
00:03:34,840 --> 00:03:38,140
Collectors use the name for particular exotic dials.

72
00:03:38,620 --> 00:03:41,580
These have distinctive details, including contrasting

73
00:03:41,580 --> 00:03:45,080
tracks and small square-ended markers inside the

74
00:03:45,080 --> 00:03:48,680
counters. Rolex itself describes the connection

75
00:03:48,680 --> 00:03:50,880
between the actor and that dial design.

76
00:03:51,900 --> 00:03:53,020
So the nickname can travel.

77
00:03:53,800 --> 00:03:55,080
Several watches can carry it.

78
00:03:55,080 --> 00:03:58,400
Personal ownership cannot travel in the same way.

79
00:03:59,200 --> 00:04:01,840
Another watch might share a reference number, a dial

80
00:04:01,840 --> 00:04:03,900
style, or a period of manufacture.

81
00:04:05,060 --> 00:04:07,680
Those similarities don't give it Newman's history.

82
00:04:08,760 --> 00:04:11,680
Keep two questions separate: what kind of watch is this,

83
00:04:12,400 --> 00:04:13,360
and whose watch was this?

84
00:04:14,300 --> 00:04:16,640
The auction would eventually bring both answers together.

85
00:04:17,400 --> 00:04:20,480
But first, the original watch moved away from Newman's

86
00:04:20,480 --> 00:04:23,280
wrist, through an exchange that had nothing to do with an

87
00:04:23,280 --> 00:04:23,960
auction catalogue.

88
00:04:25,880 --> 00:04:28,980
In the summer of 1984, James Cox was dating

89
00:04:28,980 --> 00:04:32,360
Newman's daughter Nell. He was working on a treehouse at

90
00:04:32,360 --> 00:04:33,520
the family's home in Connecticut.

91
00:04:34,500 --> 00:04:37,240
The account published by Phillips, drawing on Nell's

92
00:04:37,240 --> 00:04:40,280
letter, describes Newman asking Cox for the time.

93
00:04:41,100 --> 00:04:42,160
Cox didn't have a watch.

94
00:04:42,980 --> 00:04:43,860
Newman gave him the Daytona.

95
00:04:44,820 --> 00:04:47,220
Cox later described the exchange in his own interview.

96
00:04:48,020 --> 00:04:50,140
He remembered Newman showing him how to wind it.

97
00:04:50,640 --> 00:04:52,380
He also remembered wearing it for years.

98
00:04:53,320 --> 00:04:54,340
That's the turning point.

99
00:04:54,340 --> 00:04:57,880
For a collector looking backward, the object carries a

100
00:04:57,880 --> 00:05:01,420
famous name. For the person receiving it, the connection

101
00:05:01,420 --> 00:05:04,760
is immediate: someone he knows has handed him

102
00:05:04,760 --> 00:05:08,100
something personal. A wife gives a watch to her husband.

103
00:05:08,100 --> 00:05:11,100
The husband gives it to a young man connected to his

104
00:05:11,100 --> 00:05:13,800
family. Two gifts. One watch.

105
00:05:13,800 --> 00:05:16,960
And a history that would need to be established before

106
00:05:16,960 --> 00:05:18,180
anyone could bid on it.

107
00:05:19,880 --> 00:05:22,260
Ownership history has a name in the collecting world:

108
00:05:22,940 --> 00:05:26,360
provenance. It means the documented path an object

109
00:05:26,360 --> 00:05:29,900
has taken. Without that path, a famous-owner

110
00:05:29,900 --> 00:05:31,280
claim is just a claim.

111
00:05:32,200 --> 00:05:35,100
The Phillips lot record identified the reference, case

112
00:05:35,100 --> 00:05:38,160
number and engraving. It also listed an

113
00:05:38,160 --> 00:05:40,300
attestation letter signed by Nell Newman.

114
00:05:41,100 --> 00:05:43,760
Those details anchored the story to one physical watch.

115
00:05:44,520 --> 00:05:46,840
They matter because resemblance is easy to offer.

116
00:05:46,840 --> 00:05:50,180
A connection to a particular person needs evidence.

117
00:05:51,060 --> 00:05:54,160
Picture two visually similar objects placed side by side.

118
00:05:54,880 --> 00:05:57,600
One comes with a known family history and identifying

119
00:05:57,600 --> 00:06:01,060
details. The other comes with a promising story that you

120
00:06:01,060 --> 00:06:04,160
cannot check. The difference isn't visible in the hands

121
00:06:04,160 --> 00:06:07,660
on the dial. For this auction, the history was central

122
00:06:07,660 --> 00:06:11,080
to the offer. The next question was what bidders

123
00:06:11,080 --> 00:06:11,980
would pay for it.

124
00:06:13,800 --> 00:06:16,820
By now, the number in the opening has a more recognizable

125
00:06:16,820 --> 00:06:20,220
shape. There is the watch itself: a vintage

126
00:06:20,220 --> 00:06:22,960
racing chronograph with a distinctive dial.

127
00:06:23,820 --> 00:06:27,360
There is the association: Newman, the actor and

128
00:06:27,360 --> 00:06:29,740
driver whose name became attached to that design.

129
00:06:30,740 --> 00:06:32,960
And there is the ownership story of this particular

130
00:06:32,960 --> 00:06:36,540
example. Those layers meet in a single object.

131
00:06:37,640 --> 00:06:39,000
You can find another chronograph.

132
00:06:39,640 --> 00:06:41,180
You can find another exotic dial.

133
00:06:41,180 --> 00:06:44,200
You cannot give another watch the same past.

134
00:06:45,260 --> 00:06:46,520
That helps explain the attraction.

135
00:06:47,740 --> 00:06:50,620
It doesn't tell us exactly how much each bidder assigned

136
00:06:50,620 --> 00:06:52,100
to each part of the story.

137
00:06:52,900 --> 00:06:55,740
There is no honest equation that turns a marriage, a

138
00:06:55,740 --> 00:06:59,320
racing career and a family gift into a precise dollar

139
00:06:59,320 --> 00:07:02,560
amount. The auction supplied a price through competition.

140
00:07:03,680 --> 00:07:07,260
In October 2017, that competition began with a number

141
00:07:07,260 --> 00:07:09,680
that made the earlier estimate look very small.

142
00:07:11,480 --> 00:07:14,120
Phillips had estimated the watch at one to two million

143
00:07:14,120 --> 00:07:17,620
dollars. The first bid was ten million: five

144
00:07:17,620 --> 00:07:19,120
times the top of that estimate.

145
00:07:20,040 --> 00:07:22,580
According to the auction house, the contest lasted twelve

146
00:07:22,580 --> 00:07:24,840
minutes. A telephone bidder won.

147
00:07:25,820 --> 00:07:28,660
The published result, including the buyer's premium, was

148
00:07:28,660 --> 00:07:31,776
seventeen million, seven hundred and fifty-two thousand,

149
00:07:31,776 --> 00:07:35,220
five hundred dollars. The buyer's premium

150
00:07:35,220 --> 00:07:38,380
is the auction house's charge added to the winning bid.

151
00:07:38,380 --> 00:07:41,840
The headline number is the total result, not

152
00:07:41,840 --> 00:07:42,860
the hammer price alone.

153
00:07:43,940 --> 00:07:46,680
At the time, it was a world auction record for a

154
00:07:46,680 --> 00:07:50,020
wristwatch. The watch's history helps explain why

155
00:07:50,020 --> 00:07:53,160
bidders were interested. Their competition explains how

156
00:07:53,160 --> 00:07:53,960
the price was reached.

157
00:07:54,940 --> 00:07:57,120
It is still one sale, on one day.

158
00:07:57,840 --> 00:08:01,040
It doesn't establish what another Daytona should cost, or

159
00:08:01,040 --> 00:08:03,000
what this one would bring in a future auction.

160
00:08:05,000 --> 00:08:07,040
The sale also carried a charitable connection.

161
00:08:07,760 --> 00:08:10,400
Phillips said a portion of the proceeds would support the

162
00:08:10,400 --> 00:08:13,460
Nell Newman Foundation and Newman's Own Foundation.

163
00:08:14,500 --> 00:08:16,880
The catalogue did not give that portion a percentage.

164
00:08:17,920 --> 00:08:20,460
Newman's philanthropy had a much longer history.

165
00:08:21,080 --> 00:08:22,960
In 1982, he and A.

166
00:08:23,060 --> 00:08:26,580
E. Hotchner founded Newman's Own, a food company whose

167
00:08:26,580 --> 00:08:28,680
profits supported charitable causes.

168
00:08:29,660 --> 00:08:32,700
So the watch connects several parts of a life: work,

169
00:08:33,140 --> 00:08:35,140
racing, family and giving.

170
00:08:36,160 --> 00:08:37,720
The price makes it famous as an object.

171
00:08:38,540 --> 00:08:40,420
The two gifts make its history memorable.

172
00:08:41,420 --> 00:08:44,180
For this story, the most revealing detail is still the

173
00:08:44,180 --> 00:08:47,580
smallest. Four words on the back, from one person to

174
00:08:47,580 --> 00:08:50,100
another. Drive carefully. Me.
